Date: Mon, 8 Feb 93 11:42:29 EST
From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: POLI: Use of Force

Garret Goebel writes:
> Though on the other hand, I guess you could argue that the riots where
> the result of state initiated force... and that the riots themself where
> in some part a defensive reaction to a corrupted rule of law.  This
> doesn't explain white/black/latino violence though... or mass looting of
> private companies and grocery stores.

A particularly telling item was the the violence directed against successful
businesses run by people of Asian extraction (typically Vietnamese). And
apparently, one person arrested for looting was a lottery winner with
a guaranteed income in excess of US$100,000 per year.

Growing up in Miami, I recall several riots with similar circumstances. In
all of these occasions, the motivations seemed similar to me: a large
number of people riot and loot because they've been convinced that wealth
only flows to those who steal it and that the sight of a person with wealth
is proof of wrongdoing. And, there's always a large contingent of people who
know when lawlessness won't be punished and who therefore compute, quite
correctly, that they can get away with a certain amount of theft.

I think it takes a truly consistent anarcho-capitalist mindset to understand
that to a first approximation, and absent government intervention, wealth
is a sign of virtue, not sin.

Elias Israel
eisrael@east.sun.com

P.S. As this was my first contribution to Extropians, I hope that everyone
found it relevant and interesting.


