Date: 8 Feb 93 18:11:52 GMT
From: ssandfort@attmail.com
Subject: META: Burden of proof reform

sssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssss

Marc Ringuette wrote:

    "I intensely dislike the notion of burden of proof
    reform, because it acts to exacerbate what I see as the
    worst problems of the Extropians list:  lack of
    tolerance, flaming of anyone not toeing the party line,
    and excessive officiousness on the part of the list
    management."

I think Marc has missed the point in Tom Morrow's post and my
follow-up.  In no way would the "problems" Marc sees on the list
be exacerbated by some scheme employing a "burden of proof" onus.
Let's examine his three problems in the context of "BoP" ("burden
of proof").

LACK OF TOLERANCE--Here we go again.  If you don't accept the
existence of flying yogis--without evidence--you are intolerant.
The whole idea of BoP is give the flying yogis a fair hearing *on
their merits*.  Pretty tolerant, in my opinion.  Just intolerant
of *unsupported* claims.

FLAMING OF NAY-SAYERS--Nobody would be required to toe any party
line under a BoP system.  If they started making gratuitous
non-Extropian claims, however, the system should provide for some
sort of challenge to produce evidence.  This is not flaming.

EXCESSIVE OFFICIOUSNESS--I thought the list management was
*supposed* to manage.  BoP would not require the administrator to
intermeddle in any dispute, only to enforce list rules.  If the
rules say you may not post unsupported, non-Extropian claims,
then you shouldn't.  If you do, and are challenged, then the list
administrator would be correct in keeping your *unsupported*
claims off the net.  This is not excessive officiousness; it's
called doing the job.

I think a BoP-based system would be vastly superior to any
artificial (dare I say, flame-prone and officious?) "weekly
responder" scheme.  We don't need to build a bureaucracy to set
"misguided" newbies straight.  We do need a system to make sure
they support their non-Extropian claims.

