   I'll point out at this juncture that I've changed jobs five times since
   1987, and I've never had too much stress about it -- I'm a mercenary, and
   I go where I like. But I understand your point. However, it doesn't seem
   to apply. If one wants to sacrifice maximal economic benefit for emotional
   security, one may do so. One may, for instance, continue to patronize
   the local store, and if enough members of the community agree that this
   is a good thing it will survive in spite of the Wallmart. Or, one may
   pick a particularly stable (but boring) job. These sacrifices can be
   made if one wants to. The issue here seems to be a specious assertion
   to the effect that these sacrifices should be imposed on all of us for
   our own good.

The problem is that the choices available to one are not independent
of the choices made by another.  If enough people prefer to shop at
Wal-Mart, the other stores cease to exist, and I no longer have the
choice to pay the small premium of shopping at the traditional stores.
Etc.  The amount of economic benefit one has to sacrifice in order to
obtain security depends on the choices that other people make; thus,
people who crave security often have a strong interest in making
other people make the same choice.

Whether you consider this Good or Bad is, of course, a matter of
morality, that is, a matter of taste.

