It's nice to get to the heart of things:

   From: dkrieger@netcom.com (David Krieger)

   > Milken is hardly a political prisoner.  He was jailed for insider
   > trading, which is illegal.

   This is a remarkably naive statement.  Gandhi was jailed for making
   statements against the British occupation, which was illegal.  Solzhe-
   nitzen was jailed for writing books about the state of the Union, which
   was illegal.  Timothy Leary was jailed for bringing a minuscule amount
   of marijuana across the Mexican border, which was illegal.  Critics of
   the Shah of Iran, who were assassinated or tortured by SAVAK, were all
   indisputably guilty of the "crimes" they were charged with.

A political prisoner is someone who is jailed for advocating something
that the current regime doesn't like.  That is, it's for disseminating
politically incorrect opinions.  People who have committed other
illegal acts aren't political prisoners, even though they may have
attracted extra attention from the authorities because they *did*
advocate ideas that the regime didn't like.

Thus, of the above list, Leary was an ordinary criminal, while the
rest were political prisoners.

Of course, you can disagree with this definition, but I can't see any
other one that would be useful, unless you want to equate "political
prisoner" with "someone who is jailed under a law I disagree with".

Now, as a matter of fact, Scott Meeks was kind enough to inform me
that Milken (sp?) was convicted for various violations of SEC
regulations, involving not putting certain required information on
various SEC forms, and gave me a reference.  The rest of you seemed to
prefer to go ballistic, but not bother to inform me where I was
mistaken.

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   This is not to say that Milken did not commit some technical violations,
   but they were not precisely what you would refer to as terrible things.
   Of course, even the most minor securities violation will likely put you
   behind bars for as long as a murderer...

Milken ran into the classical problem:

    You can get away with being obnoxious.
    You can get away with breaking the law.
    But you can't get away with doing both!
    -- Caroline Roos

If you start doing something that annoys people in power, you have to
be very careful to color inside the lines.  One of the problems these
days is that "people in power" can be almost anybody -- Leona Helmsley
got turned in because she pissed off her employees.  Also, there was
that fine quote about "Taxes are for little people" that worked its
way into the trial -- Big Mistake.

Ideally, the laws are explicit as to exactly what's legal and what's
illegal.  But I've never seen a society or group of any sort that has
managed to succeed at making that distinction.  And there are always
people working hard to explore the grey area, and use the ambiguities
to extract some benefit or other, often in ways that the law was
intended to prevent.  If you want to be safe, stay out of the grey
areas; but of course, that will cost you some potential profits as
well.

